WEBVTT

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:06.280
Watch me, Dad.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:09.780
I’ll be starting a revolution!

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.290
I wonder what connection
Kawachi’s bread has to his father...

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:20.540
It seems that reminding him of his father has had a good effect on him.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:22.970
Yeah. Up until now,
his admiration for his father

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:25.290
was probably nothing more than a memory of the past.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:32.180
But now that he’s decided to take over his father’s dream of creating a revolutionary bread,

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.310
he’s probably feeling his father’s presence closer to him than before.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:37.900
Occasionally, for a man,

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:43.060
the dream of surpassing your father is absolutely necessary for you to grow.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:46.520
I see. That’s what it is.

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:47.740
All right!

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.020
With this, both of them have begun baking.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:59.410
There’s English bread, German bread, and French bread,

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:01.290
but Japan’s bread, <i>Japan</i>, does not exist.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:03.540
In that case, there’s no choice but to create it.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:07.000
This story is a serious, biographical ballad of a boy who possesses Solar Hands,

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:12.260
Azuma Kazuma, who will create a Japanese bread made by and for the Japanese people,

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:15.640
which can be presented to the world proudly.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:49.690
The Great Detective Pierrot!!

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:51.650
A reaction you lay your life on!

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:55.490
That smells good.

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:56.240
I wonder what it is...

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:57.780
What could it be?

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.740
Shachihoko-san’s bread is really fragrant.

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:05.370
It’s the smell of danger for Kawachi.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.250
Manager... C-Could this be...?

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:09.500
Yeah, perhaps.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.500
I believe that everybody has begun to notice by now...

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:17.260
The wonderful fragrance
wafting over from Spencer’s bread.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:21.180
Actually, this is an ingredient
that everybody in Japan knows of.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.020
This fragrance is miso.
Note: Miso (<b>味噌) is bean paste.</b>

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:24.140
Miso?!

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:25.890
N-Now that you mention it...

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:29.390
However... This isn’t any ordinary miso.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:33.400
This is the legendary miso that
even most Japanese don’t know of...

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:37.360
It’s kodaimai-miso!
Note: Kodaimai (<b>古代米) refers to an old strain of rice.</b>

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:38.690
Kodaimai-miso?!

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:41.950
Uh... What’s that?

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:43.700
That is...

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:47.710
Could it be miso made using the ancient red rice?

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:50.000
E-Exactly!

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.500
Oh, I thought so.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:54.710
How unusual.
You know about it, Azuma-san?

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:56.840
How mean, Tsukino...

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:59.210
You do know that my family grows rice, right?

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:01.430
Now that you mention it, I kind of...

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:02.720
Remember that.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:06.270
You see, “kodaimai” is a general term

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:10.310
for pure breeds of rice that have been developed from wild rice.

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:14.610
Amongst these breeds, red rice is famous because sekihan was developed from it.

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:17.780
In addition to being more
nutritious than modern rice strains,

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:19.990
it grows well without pesticides,

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:22.470
and is strong against drought and cold weather.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:25.320
That’s why it used to be grown all over Japan,

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.290
but when you compare it to selectively bred modern rice strains,

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:31.830
it yields less than half of the normal quantity of crop.

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:34.960
So, it isn’t cultivated very much these days.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:39.420
What? Were you guys listening to me?

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:41.970
Huh? Oh, yeah.

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:46.140
I guess it’s true that
everyone’s an expert in something...

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:50.480
I’m sure that his grandfather has
told him this tens of thousands of times.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.270
It’s done!

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:55.190
This is my masterpiece, made with kodaimai-miso!

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:57.810
Alexandria!

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:06.080
You’re really fast, Spencer, because Kawachi has just started his baking.

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:07.820
That’s to be expected.

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:10.710
This Alexandria’s an Egyptian bread.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.960
It doesn’t take long to rise, so it’s obvious that it’ll be done quickly.

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.040
I’d like for you to eat it while it’s still hot.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:20.380
Then, I’ll begin the judging right away.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:21.550
Please.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:25.760
I know that I’m supposed to
be concentrating on my bread,

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:28.050
but right now, it’s in the middle of baking.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.770
So, I can’t help but wonder what Pierrot’s reaction will be.

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:36.650
What’s with Pierrot?

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:38.570
He isn’t moving at all.

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:42.070
It looks as though time has stopped.

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:43.870
Hey, there.

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:44.990
Who’re you?

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:48.580
Tonegawa Conan. I’m a detective.

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:54.210
B... By Conan, do you mean the show
that airs on Monday during prime time?

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:55.210
That’s right.

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:57.750
If you’re to make an appearance in a program from another broadcasting station,

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:59.540
wouldn’t it cause trouble?

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:01.220
It’s all right.

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:05.350
I mean, though my name may be Conan,
I’m not the real thing.

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:07.180
As long as I apologize, it’ll be all right.

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:10.640
This ain’t something that can be resolved by just an apology, right?

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:11.890
Shut up will you, Baldy.

00:06:11.890 --> 00:06:13.850
More importantly, haven’t you noticed something?

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:15.060
Y-Yeah, I guess...

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:16.940
Hey, who you calling Baldy?!

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:20.190
Go on, look around you.

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:30.450
It certainly looks like nobody’s moving at all.

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:33.080
It’s almost like time has stopped.

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:35.040
Exactly.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.080
This is the realm of Pierrot’s thoughts.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:42.340
In other words, we’ve been sent
into a dimension created by Pierrot.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:43.420
What was that?!

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:46.180
In order to get out of this dimension,

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:51.430
it’s essential that you deduce the answer
that Pierrot, the creator of this place, seeks.

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:53.390
The answer that Pierrot seeks?

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:56.350
Oh! Take a look at that!

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:57.610
That old man!

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:00.690
H-He’s about to stab that person!

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:03.530
Even though this person is right-handed,

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:08.150
by holding the knife in his left hand, he’s trying to create a fake alibi as he murders his wife.

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:12.700
See? His holding his chopsticks
in his right hand is proof of this.

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:13.660
Definitely.

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:16.080
But... Why’s he holding chopsticks?

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:18.590
Amazing, right?

00:07:18.590 --> 00:07:20.000
My powers of deduction...

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:21.090
Uh... yeah...

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:22.420
Oh, look at that girl there.

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:24.260
Tsukino?

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:26.430
“Why is it that a cute girl like me

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:29.600
is getting less time on the screen, and
why am I not treated more like a heroine...?”

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:30.930
...is what she’s thinking.

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:34.390
Why do I get the feeling that you’re right...?

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:36.810
Nothing’s too difficult for my great deductive powers.

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:37.690
That man there...

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:41.020
He’s frustrated because he hasn’t been able to do reactions or make a commentary

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:42.650
for the longest time.

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:44.570
V-Very true.

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:50.650
The guy with the hair is thinking,
“Without me, <i>Japan</i> would fall.”

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:55.120
But isn’t this mind-reading and not deduction?

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:56.830
And look at the mushroom-head.

00:07:56.830 --> 00:07:58.330
He doesn’t look very happy, does he?

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:01.210
And now, that was merely an observation.

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:03.370
Well, thanks to my appearance,

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:06.220
it’s certain that the ratings today will increase by three times the usual.

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:07.010
Dumbass.

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:08.960
Don’t get all cocky.

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:15.600
No, my deductions are always 100% accurate.

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:18.060
I’m really a genius, aren’t I?

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:21.230
Just as you’d expect of a popular show aired during peak hours.

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:24.610
Hey, there.

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:27.280
Enough with the self-flattery, okay?

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:29.110
Nobody’ll like you when you grow up, you know...

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:32.110
Oh! What did you just say?!

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:33.370
Huh?

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:34.790
Nobody’ll like you when you grow up...

00:08:34.790 --> 00:08:35.490
Before that.

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:39.120
I said that you were flattering yourself.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:41.330
Whom are you referring to here?

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:43.800
Your name was Conan, right?

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:45.010
I’m talking about you.

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:47.470
I’m saying that Conan was singing his own praises.

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:48.130
Huh?

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:50.010
Conan was singing his own praises...

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:52.720
Conan was singing his own praises?

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:56.020
Conan was singing his own praises!

00:08:56.020 --> 00:08:56.640
What was that?

00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:58.100
Conan was singing his own praises.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:08:59.020
Conan temaemiso.

00:08:59.020 --> 00:09:00.810
Conan temaemiso! Conan temaemiso!

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:01.810
Co...

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:05.770
Kodaimai-miso!

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:09.320
Exactly!

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:13.530
That was exactly the answer I was seeking.

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:22.330
Oh? Everyone’s moving!

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:24.250
I-I’m back to the real world...

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:25.750
Congratulations.

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:28.250
This is for you, in celebration of your escape.

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:29.840
Yay!

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:31.930
Geez, what are you doing?

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:33.510
I mean, what’s with you?

00:09:34.300 --> 00:09:37.850
We’re in the middle of the match,
so don’t involve me in your reactions.

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:42.770
P-Please don’t get angry like that.

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:49.190
Because I’ve... been...
sufficiently punished for my sins...

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:05.040
Pierrot!

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:16.550
Pierrot!

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:20.510
W-What happened, Kawachi?!

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:22.980
I-I don’t know!

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:26.020
But... Pierrot’s suddenly been stabbed...

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:27.480
In the back?

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:29.270
A clean stab with a survival knife...

00:10:29.780 --> 00:10:30.990
Kawachi!

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:31.950
It can’t be that you...?

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:33.280
What was that?!

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:35.870
You mean, Kawachi killed Pierrot?!

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:37.910
Of-Of course not!

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:39.360
What are you talking about?!

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.870
I have no reason to kill him!

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:47.330
But, the only person who could’ve
stabbed Pierrot in the back is you.

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:50.080
T-That’s true, but...

00:10:50.510 --> 00:10:51.970
You did it, didn’t you?!

00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:53.340
Confess already!

00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:57.760
It was in the heat of the moment, and...

00:10:57.760 --> 00:10:58.810
Pierrot?

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:00.050
Hello there.

00:11:00.050 --> 00:11:01.270
You moron!

00:11:01.270 --> 00:11:05.060
I’ve forgiven all of the mean
horseplay you’ve pulled up until now,

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:06.930
but this time, you’ve gone too far!

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:09.110
It ain’t no horseplay.

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:10.940
This was an assassination.

00:11:11.650 --> 00:11:12.940
Cut the crap, will you?

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:18.450
L... L-Look here. Y-You seem to
be mistaken about something, so...

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:20.580
I’ll make it clear.

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:24.660
I’ve really been stabbed.

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:27.370
It’s just that I’m durable, so I’m still alive.

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:28.460
Like that’s possible...

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:32.380
When you become a world-level clown of my skill,

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:36.840
it’s obvious that terrorists and spies
from various countries will try to kill you.

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:40.840
Since I usually dodge these attempts
skillfully, I guess you folks never noticed.

00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:44.170
In just this tournament,

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:47.690
I’ve been on the receiving end of many hundreds of terrorist attacks.

00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:53.360
It’s so stupid, I don’t know what to say.

00:11:57.150 --> 00:12:00.610
While we were going along with your
little joke, my bread finished baking.

00:12:02.830 --> 00:12:05.660
This is my revolutionary dinner bread.

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:07.370
This is Victory!

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:09.330
Oh, it smells good.

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:13.170
Listen up. Leave that stupid story aside for now,

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:15.260
and judge it while it’s hot.

00:12:15.260 --> 00:12:16.670
Roger dodger.

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:21.930
Well then, let’s dig in mambo.

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:24.180
The year 199X

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:26.390
A charity performance?

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:29.850
And in Libya?!

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:30.730
The Ex-ringmaster

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:33.400
Well, you’re the chief now,

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:34.570
so do whatever you like.

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:38.780
However, I won’t give you a single penny for it.

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:40.070
Don’s changed.

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:41.820
And so have all of the other performers.

00:12:50.750 --> 00:12:51.830
That’s right.

00:12:51.830 --> 00:12:55.170
After the much-awaited performance in Paris became a huge success,

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:57.330
Quedam became a rich circus,

00:12:57.330 --> 00:13:01.800
and all of the performers
stopped practicing and became lazy.

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:04.720
We don’t really need to practice.

00:13:04.720 --> 00:13:06.680
We’re stars!

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:09.350
We can spend the rest of our lives in tranquility.

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:11.440
Things can’t go on this way.

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:12.690
What was that?!

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:14.310
A charity performance?

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:16.980
And why do we have to do it in Libya?!

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:19.690
The situation there’s really unstable.

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:22.320
There’s got to be safer places we can go to.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:26.790
We’ve finally gotten rich enough that we can spend the rest of our lives in comfort,

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:30.910
so why must we put our lives at risk?

00:13:31.870 --> 00:13:32.870
For love!

00:13:33.380 --> 00:13:34.620
Love?!

00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:37.380
I want to be like the Reverend King.

00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:38.530
He said...

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:40.010
“With his message of love, the Reverend King opposed racial discrimination”

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:43.840
We must rise to the majestic heights
of meeting physical force with soul force.

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:45.970
Violence breeds only violence.

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:46.160
“With his message of love, the Reverend King opposed racial discrimination”

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:52.940
That is why, through the power of
the universal language of laughter,

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:56.230
I wish for Libya and France to make amends.

00:13:56.230 --> 00:13:59.900
For a circus, laughter is... the very essence of love.

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:03.490
I don’t want to lose that.

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:16.420
Alone again.

00:14:16.420 --> 00:14:20.590
I guess nobody would join me
on such a reckless performance.

00:14:23.470 --> 00:14:26.180
I guess you’re like me, huh?

00:14:27.350 --> 00:14:28.390
That’s right.

00:14:28.390 --> 00:14:32.310
I guess I can’t force everyone to play along with my whims.

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:40.480
After the performance in Paris, I became really famous.

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:42.940
Yet, my parents never appeared.

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:49.660
I’m sure that’s because they despise the dirty, money-tainted person that I’ve become.

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:52.040
That’s why I must be reborn...

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:53.540
Like the Reverend King,

00:14:53.540 --> 00:14:57.040
I must be reborn...
as a person pure of heart.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:14:59.750
Chief.

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:03.170
You’re late.

00:15:03.170 --> 00:15:05.380
We all got here before you, and we’re tired of waiting.

00:15:08.510 --> 00:15:09.180
Guys!

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:10.510
Chief!

00:15:14.390 --> 00:15:15.440
I’m sorry.

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:19.900
I still... I still have a place to return to.

00:15:19.900 --> 00:15:22.230
I couldn’t be happier.

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:23.900
You guys understand me, don’t you?

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:27.990
At that time, I thanked God for the fact
that everyone still had love in their hearts.

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:33.410
1 hour later
At that time, I thanked God for the fact
that everyone still had love in their hearts.

00:15:34.020 --> 00:15:34.750
But...

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:38.670
In the beginning, we just couldn’t get permission to perform,

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:42.170
so we had no choice but to
perform using guerilla tactics.

00:15:43.420 --> 00:15:45.250
Pierrot, incoming!

00:15:45.790 --> 00:15:48.510
2 hours later

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:50.720
What’s with him?

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:52.680
He’s talking of nothing but Libya.

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:54.220
When’s this going to be done?

00:15:54.220 --> 00:15:56.510
I really want to say something...

00:15:56.810 --> 00:16:00.020
And I think it was at our 16th performance.

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:04.860
Finally, the supreme leader of
Libya, the colonel, came to watch us.

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:10.200
3 hours later

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:14.700
I-Is this story really going to continue for much longer...?

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:21.540
However, there was a group of people who
didn’t take kindly to our message of peace.

00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:29.800
So, we were attacked many times by
terrorists trying to stop us from performing.

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:31.340
We’ll put a stop to it.

00:16:31.340 --> 00:16:32.680
We’re putting a stop to it!

00:16:33.140 --> 00:16:35.680
I’m sorry but this is war.

00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:42.190
4 hours later

00:16:42.520 --> 00:16:46.690
Because it’s the all-important finals,
I’ve been silently listening, but...

00:16:46.990 --> 00:16:48.650
Oh, and there was that...

00:16:48.650 --> 00:16:52.910
But now, Libya’s a peaceful country and we’re happy.

00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:57.660
He used me for his reaction to my opponent.

00:16:58.330 --> 00:16:59.660
He called me a murderer,

00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:03.160
and then he’s been talking about some stupid Libya story...

00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:06.210
I’m at my limit!

00:17:07.460 --> 00:17:09.340
Cut the crap, will you!

00:17:09.340 --> 00:17:13.680
You aren’t judging my bread, and you’re just telling us some stupid story...

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:17.890
F-Finally, Kawachi.

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:19.890
I was waiting for you to comment.

00:17:21.690 --> 00:17:24.270
It isn’t just some stupid story.

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:26.230
It’s the story of Libya and me.

00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:31.490
The story of Libya and me.

00:17:33.070 --> 00:17:35.610
Like I’m going to be fooled by that performance again.

00:17:38.830 --> 00:17:39.620
Pierrot!

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:40.500
An ambulance!

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:41.620
Call an ambulance!

00:17:42.290 --> 00:17:44.580
Libya and me.

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:47.130
Libya and me?

00:17:47.550 --> 00:17:50.340
Libya and me.

00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:52.840
Boku to Libya.

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:58.720
It’s Victory!

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:00.730
Ah, that’s right.

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:01.310
Victory.

00:18:02.190 --> 00:18:04.230
Congratulations, Kawachi.

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:05.940
You’ve won.

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:07.320
It’s your victory.

00:18:07.320 --> 00:18:09.190
I-I lost?!

00:18:09.190 --> 00:18:10.560
How can that be?!

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:16.700
Unfortunately, it looks like I no longer
have the energy to comment on that.

00:18:17.740 --> 00:18:19.540
S-So...

00:18:19.540 --> 00:18:23.580
I’ll leave the commentary on Kawachi’s bread to him...

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:25.580
Kuro-yan!

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:29.920
Okay. Very well.

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:31.300
I shall explain.

00:18:31.300 --> 00:18:34.090
Kawachi’s strategy can be
broadly divided into three parts.

00:18:34.590 --> 00:18:37.350
The first is the dattan sobayu.

00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:41.600
Instead of water, he used the nutritious sobayu in his dough.

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:44.270
Then, second is the bread mold.

00:18:44.270 --> 00:18:45.600
It’s shaped like a V.

00:18:48.400 --> 00:18:53.530
Normally, to allow the dough to expand while baking,

00:18:53.530 --> 00:18:57.320
the top of a regular baking mold is
about 0.5cm wider than the bottom.

00:18:58.120 --> 00:19:03.000
The wider it is, the more the bread can expand.

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:04.870
This makes the bread even more delicious.

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:08.070
Kawachi actually made the top of the bread mold 3cm wide,

00:19:08.070 --> 00:19:10.580
which is six times wider than usual.

00:19:11.340 --> 00:19:13.880
Wait, if you did something like that...

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:14.760
Yeah.

00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:16.550
Normally, if you did that,

00:19:16.550 --> 00:19:21.590
the top of the bread would become heavy,
thus collapsing on itself and getting crushed.

00:19:21.590 --> 00:19:23.600
But Kawachi pulled it off.

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:28.100
By using a trick, his third one, on
the dinner breads ears... no, its heart,

00:19:28.650 --> 00:19:30.600
with this croissant dough.

00:19:31.190 --> 00:19:32.850
S-Such a strategy...!

00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:34.150
That’s right.

00:19:34.150 --> 00:19:38.820
He surrounded the dinner bread
dough with the hard croissant dough,

00:19:38.820 --> 00:19:40.360
and prevented it from collapsing on itself.

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:41.870
I see.

00:19:41.870 --> 00:19:45.250
There was a mountain of
dinner-bread molds in his room.

00:19:45.830 --> 00:19:47.420
I suppose he was looking for

00:19:47.420 --> 00:19:51.750
the maximum angle that would allow
the bread to expand without collapsing.

00:19:52.550 --> 00:19:57.260
And, his biggest trick was using
black pig lard in the croissant dough.

00:19:57.930 --> 00:20:02.600
Unlike regular pigs, black
pigs are fed a high quality feed.

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:06.720
They are also left to graze freely
and live a relatively stress-free life.

00:20:07.190 --> 00:20:10.230
The lard has a mild sweetness to it,

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:13.020
and is high in unsaturated fatty acids,

00:20:13.020 --> 00:20:16.610
giving you a top quality lard that’s also low in cholesterol.

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:20.120
Thus, Kawachi’s bread was amazing.

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:23.620
That is why Pierrot laid his life
on the line for his reaction to it.

00:20:24.790 --> 00:20:26.660
It’s my complete loss.

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:31.620
Talk about amazing.

00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:36.800
T-That was a perfect commentary.

00:20:37.670 --> 00:20:40.050
With this, I can relax, and...

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:42.600
Pierrot. Hey, Pierrot!

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:43.640
Where’s that ambulance?!

00:20:48.560 --> 00:20:49.390
Pierrot!

00:20:50.230 --> 00:20:51.520
You’re a moron!

00:20:51.520 --> 00:20:53.640
Why risk your life for a stupid reaction?!

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:57.490
I guess you wouldn’t understand.

00:20:57.490 --> 00:21:01.700
But, before I’m a judge, I’m a clown.

00:21:02.820 --> 00:21:05.870
Even at the cost of my life, happy things must...

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:07.500
I understand!

00:21:07.500 --> 00:21:09.460
I understand, so don’t talk anymore.

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:11.370
Kawachi...

00:21:11.370 --> 00:21:12.540
W-What is it?

00:21:12.540 --> 00:21:15.250
I-It was delicious.

00:21:15.250 --> 00:21:19.550
The bread of your father’s dreams.

00:21:21.590 --> 00:21:26.890
I... I also... want to meet... my father...

00:21:35.900 --> 00:21:36.820
Kawachi...

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:38.480
Are you all right?

00:21:39.530 --> 00:21:40.990
Now that I think about it...

00:21:40.990 --> 00:21:44.200
The reason he does stupid things like that...

00:21:44.200 --> 00:21:51.490
I think it’s because he hopes
that it’ll help his parents find him.

00:21:51.890 --> 00:21:52.950
Kawachi...

00:21:55.250 --> 00:21:56.000
He...

00:21:56.540 --> 00:22:00.170
He understood my father’s feelings, and yet...

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:07.510
And yet, I... never understood his
feelings, and always just got mad at him...

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:12.140
Don’t cry, Kawachi.

00:22:12.980 --> 00:22:15.520
I wonder if Pierrot-san’s all right?

00:22:15.900 --> 00:22:19.480
By the looks of things, even a clown might be...

00:22:25.490 --> 00:22:26.990
Father!

00:22:28.660 --> 00:22:30.330
It’s me, Father!

00:22:30.330 --> 00:22:30.700
What are you doing!

00:22:30.700 --> 00:22:34.000
Father!
What are you doing!

00:24:05.350 --> 00:24:08.970
Throwing my life away for a bread examination sure seems stupid.

00:24:08.970 --> 00:24:11.760
It's a laugh riot. And the last thing I'm seeing is that clown.

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:15.310
I just wish I could've seen my parents again before I died...

00:24:15.310 --> 00:24:16.320
Next time, on Yakitate!! Japan,

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:18.700
Reunion at the VIP Seat!! Heaven Again!

00:24:18.700 --> 00:24:19.900
Be sure to watch!

00:24:20.470 --> 00:24:22.650
Yakitate Seeds of Knowledge!!

00:24:23.250 --> 00:24:26.310
Black pigs are original inhabitants of the Berkshire region of England.

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:29.700
Compared to normal pigs, they have far more amino and nucleic acid material.

00:24:29.700 --> 00:24:31.670
That uniqueness makes them all the more tasty!

00:24:32.150 --> 00:24:34.520
Ehhh??!
